    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Enrich changed products into their own source\nA product changes in the graph, the workflow reads it, a language model writes a description, and the description goes back into the graph as a record in a **separate source**, `enrichment`.\n\n- **When the graph changes** gets one item per changed entry: its tag `key`, `hash` and `action`. The webhook carries the event, never the data.\n- **Drop redeliveries** skips an entry of a delivery the sender retried (same `deliveryId` and `key`), so a retry doesn't enrich twice.\n- **Only changed products** keeps upserted `ProductCard` entries (see the loop guard below).\n- **Only this workflow's manifest** keeps the entries under the manifest the *Enterspeed Context Graph account* credential reads, which **Read the manifest** gives it. Publishing a manifest announces every entry under it, so without this a publish would enrich the whole catalogue, twice while two manifests run side by side. When you move the credential to a new manifest, the filter moves with it.\n- **One product at a time** takes them one by one, so each description stays matched to its product.\n- **Read the changed product** reads that shape: the tag key without its `{manifest}:shape:` prefix is the shape key.\n- **Enrich** asks the chat model for one sentence.\n- **Write to the enrichment source** pushes a `productEnrichment` record, keyed by the product's id, to the `enrichment` source. The schema reads it as the `ProductEnrichment` shape.\n\n**One entry failing doesn't stop the others.** A read, a model call or a write that fails, or a record the graph refuses, goes to *Entries that weren't enriched* with its tag key and the error; every other entry is still written and the run succeeds.",
        "height": 620,
        "width": 720
      },
      "name": "About",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -20,
        -640
      ],
      "id": "84d2047d-bada-5866-8f37-bbee035c9882"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Why the enrichment has its own source (the loop guard)\nThe workflow writes to the graph, and every write to the graph sends this webhook again. Without a guard, each enrichment would start another run, forever.\n\nThe envelope says **which entries** changed, never **who** changed them, so the workflow can't recognise its own writes from the event. It can recognise **what** changed: the enrichment is a separate source with its own model and shape (`ProductEnrichment`), and `ProductModel` can't read it: a model has one root and no joins. A write to `enrichment` changes only `ProductEnrichment` entries, and the filter keeps only `ProductCard` entries, so that run ends at the filter and writes nothing. The two meet in a representation, which composes them when it is read and sends no webhook.\n\nKeep it that way: if the filter ever lets through a shape of the enrichment's own model, the guard stops working. On a deployed graph you can also give the webhook subscription a filter, `{\"prefixes\": [\"shape:ProductCard\"]}` (prefixes leave out the manifest id), so those deliveries aren't sent at all.",
        "height": 440,
        "width": 760,
        "color": 3
      },
      "name": "Loop guard",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        740,
        -640
      ],
      "id": "d8fe744e-229e-5df2-9bb5-0cc388ed0597"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Before you run it\n- **A dev-server with the `catalogue` and `enrichment` sources**: `npm install` then `npm run serve` in this repo's `e2e` folder (Node 26). CI runs this workflow against it, with a stand-in chat model, on every pull request.\n- **Enterspeed Context Graph account** points at the graph, **Enterspeed Context Graph webhook** holds the webhook subscription's signing secret. Against the dev-server, start it with `webhookUrl` (this trigger's production URL) and `webhookSecret` (`npm run serve` sends its webhook on to this trigger's production URL, signed with the secret `scripts/setup.mjs` stored in n8n); against a deployed graph, register the URL as a webhook subscription and use its secret. Nothing else in the workflow changes.\n- **OpenAI account**: your API key, Base URL `https://api.openai.com/v1`. Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint works through its Base URL; for another provider, swap *Chat model* for its chat model node.\n- **Publish (activate) the workflow**: the trigger only listens on its production URL once it is published.",
        "height": 300,
        "width": 720,
        "color": 7
      },
      "name": "Setup",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -20,
        360
      ],
      "id": "5a6466f8-753a-5545-a504-57939b734aec"
    }
  ],
